{"id": 375358, "name": "DANFORTH & CARTER v. TIMMERMAN", "name_abbreviation": "Danforth v. Timmerman", "decision_date": "1903-02-25", "docket_number": "", "first_page": "259", "last_page": "260", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "65 S.C. 259"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "3 Strob., 542", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Strob.", "case_ids": [8487033], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/scl/34/0542-01"]}, {"cite": "2 Bail., 163", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Bail.", "case_ids": [2513608, 2509037], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/scl/18/0163-01", "/scl/18/0163-02"]}, {"cite": "4 Strob., 214", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Strob.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "2 Hill, 294", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Hill,", "case_ids": [11253702], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/scl/20/0294-01"]}, {"cite": "12 S. C., 56", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [361246], "pin_cites": [{"page": "130"}], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/12/0056-01"]}, {"cite": "42 S. C., 511", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4402482], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/42/0511-01"]}, {"cite": "1 Strob. Eq., 262", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Strob. Eq.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "27 S. C., 318", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4387990], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/27/0318-01"]}, {"cite": "1 Rich., 502", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Rich.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "4 Strob., 214", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Strob.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "1 Rich., 502", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Rich.", "opinion_index": 0}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 235, "char_count": 3197, "ocr_confidence": 0.651, "pagerank": {"raw": 8.074270406899607e-08, "percentile": 0.4668606161980872}, "sha256": "e4d6a06345b50528637527c3ced25e352422b594cb481271a7cd35da3de72d1f", "simhash": "1:838ef88a8cf44c9b", "word_count": 545}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T17:58:05.773230+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["DANFORTH & CARTER v. TIMMERMAN."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Jones.\nThis was an action in a magistrate\u2019s court upon an account for merchandise. The defendant demurred for defect of parties defendant in that the summons was addressed to James A. Timmerman, and the verified account was against James A. Timmerman, agent; the magistrate sustained the demurrer and dismissed the complaint, but .further provided that plaintiff be allowed to amend his complaint by making Mrs. James A. Timmerman a party defendant, if so advised. On appeal to the Circuit Court, the order of the magistrate was reversed and the case remanded for trial, from which order defendant now appeals.\nWe think there was no error. The pleadings alleged that defendant is indebted to plaintiff on account for said merchandise, and the plaintiff assumes the burden of showing the defendant is person\u00e1lly liable therefor. The bare fact that the account was stated as against James A. Timmerman, agent, is not material under the issue as now presented. The case does not show who was defendant\u2019s principal in the transaction, if, indeed, the goods were purchased by defendant as agent for some one. If it should appear that defendant purchased and received the goods as agent for another, and the goods were charged to defendant as agent, defendant may, nevertheless, be personally liable, if at the time of the purchase of the goods his principal was not disclosed or known to plaintiff, or if defendant had no authority to bind his principal in the transaction, or if, 'notwithstanding the agency, he voluntarily incurred a personal responsibility to pay for the goods received by him. Miller v. Ford, 4 Strob., 214, 1 Ency. Law, 2 ed., p. 1122. In an action brought to render an agent personally liable for goods purchased by him and charged to him as agent, the onus lies upon defendant to show that another is exclusively liable. Miller v. Stock, 2 Bailey, 163. The case of Ligon v. Irvine, 1 Rich., 502, does not apply to this case as now presented, since in that suit it appeared that the contract was expressly made in behalf of a disclosed principal. This case was properly remanded to the magistrate for trial.\nThe judgment of the Circuit Court is affirmed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Jones."}], "attorneys": ["Mr. B. B. Bvans, for appellant,", "Mr. B. B. Nicholson, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "DANFORTH & CARTER v. TIMMERMAN.\nDemurrer \u2014 Parties\u2014Principa!, and Agent. \u2014 It is not ground of demurrer for defect of parties defendant that magistrate summons is addressed to the defendant, and the itemized account attached is charged to him as agent.\nLig\u00f3n v. Irvine, 1 Rich., 502, distinguished from this.\nBefore Ardrich, J., Edgefield, August, 1902.\nAffirmed.\nAction in magistrate court by Danforth & Carter against James A. Timmerman. From order on Circuit reversing order of magistrate sustaining demurrer, defendant appeals.\nMr. B. B. Bvans, for appellant,\ncites: On main question: 2 N. & McC., 70; 27 S. C., 318; 1 Strob. Eq., 262; 1 Rich. D\u201e 502; Story on Ag., 193 ; Code, 149 ; 42 S. C., 511. Demur- \u25a0 \u25a0rer is remedy: 12 S. C., 56, 130; Code, 165-6.\nMr. B. B. Nicholson, contra,\ncites: On main question: 2 Kent\u2019s Com., 493; 2 Hill, 294; 1 McM., 352; 4 Strob., 214; 1 Ency., 392; 4 McC., 392; 2 Bail., 163; 3 Strob., 542; 1 Ency., 403, note.\nFebruary 25, 1903."}, "file_name": "0259-01", "first_page_order": 267, "last_page_order": 268}